format session-log-v1
stamp 20260826.134505
editor Claude Code
model claude-opus-5
voice Kyri
host Vultr SEA NixOS VPS ai-jail pier (Daylight DC-1 hand) -- Eastern (EDT)
title the wound the census took for a plant, and the arrival nobody guarded
prompt Autonomous loop -- pull xykj61 first, roster cold, Standfast on reds, next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, rota row N mod 5, send each finished increment
think The round opened on a dirty index: the prior lap ended at git add with a complete, coherent package staged and never committed. A cold roster open refuses exactly that under run_verdict=lap_unclosed, which is REDS %188's signature and the guard working rather than failing. Read the whole staged diff, ran tools/s/standing_equipment_witness.rish green at 46 behaviors, then the full roster --hot: 68 of 68 green, tree_moved=no, run_verdict=ok. Committed it as the lap that had ended at staging.
think Then the round-open pull. xykj61 had moved ten commits of its own from the macOS bench, so the rebase replayed ten of mine onto it. One conflict across all ten, the shared Git nib line on the living card, which is the union-on-shared-pins case the Grain foundation already names. Took the replayed side each time since the nib is re-pinned in the work commit at round close anyway.
think Council rota row 0 at the open -- N was 3490, prior lap read 4, so 4 -> 0 advances by one cleanly. Aether, Jupiter, why the work exists, the sense of hearing. The compass says witness before narrative; Lindy says a checked claim stays audible while an asserted one fades; the Grain says a guard that cannot red guards nothing, so its silence must be earned. Three seats, one note, and it turned out to be this lap's finding rather than a frame around it.
obs The rebase moved N from 3490 to 3493, so a naive re-read at close would say row 3 and skip rows 1 and 2. That is a NEW cause for the rota skew the seed already measures -- the seed names "a lap makes two commits or none," and this is "a round-open pull brings another pier's commits forward." The proposed fix, advance by lap rather than by commit, already covers it and waits on Keaton's word. Surfaced rather than re-booked.
think The finding came from obeying the recipe. The loop prompt names three road-ahead documents; the second, active-designing/date/20260816/20260816-205859_double-seat-expansion-eight-chapters.md, was not there. tools/d/dated_path_resolve.rish answered verdict=missing. The file on disk is ..._eight-seasons.md -- the chapter molt of 20260826 swept season to chapter across living prose and rewrote a PATH LITERAL whose dated basename correctly kept the elder word. Four living sites, two of them in tools/l/launch-claude-chapter.rish, the script that starts every unattended lap.
think Same class as REDS %265 one file over, so a lantern firing twice. The question worth the lap was why no standing guard caught it. tracked_link_scan.sh reads ](...) syntax at line 100 and these are bare literals. phantom_path_scan.sh passes "a literal resolving NOWHERE" free BY NAMED RULE, delegating to the dated-path census. So the census is the one guard that sees the class.
think First hypothesis was that the total ratchet absorbed it -- refs_lost=168 against lost_ceiling=168, zero slack. Measurement refused that story: repairing all four sites moved nothing at all. Not the ceiling. The references were never in the corpus.
think Rooted it by experiment rather than by reading. Broke the seed alone: broken_gone 151 -> 152, refs_lost 168 -> 169, under_ceiling=no. Broke the launcher alone: refs_total and refs_home each fell by one and every other field held -- the reference left the accounting entirely. dp_discovered_fixture_basenames emits any dated basename whose SPRIG names no tracked file, which is precisely the definition of a broken reference, so it took the wound for a planted fixture. The subtraction then works BY BASENAME across every file, which is why one bad path in one .rish erased the identical break in the .md beside it. That is why both-broken read the same as neither-broken.
obs The instrument could not tell a plant from a wound because it used the wound's own signature as the plant's test. That is the vacuum law one turn on: a guard proven able to red can still be blinded by its own exemption.
think Tried the crisp fix first -- a plant fabricates its stamp, a wound misremembers a sprig on a real one -- and measurement killed it: 28 of the plants reuse real tracked stamps, including 20260101-000000. So the honest property is who names it. A plant is planted by an instrument.
think Narrowed discovery to the three names this tree already calls instruments, _control, _witness, _scan, in any of its four languages. Measured the cost before writing it: 68 discovered pairs across 26 files, 50 from controls, and exactly five names lost, all test-body strings in tools/rye/session_logs_archive.rye and scribe/reader.rye. Checked each of the five in context to be sure they were plants rather than wounds the instrument had been hiding -- all five sit inside std.testing.expect or is_scribe_extension calls. Declared them by hand, which is the shape the exclusions file already asks for.
obs Included _witness.rye deliberately after measuring that it discovers zero new names. Without it no .rye is ever read for plants, and the control's // comment-strip leg would have passed vacuously. Moved that pen file from writer.rye to writer_witness.rye rather than leave a leg that cannot red -- repairing a leg my own change would have hollowed out is the same discipline as proving a new one.
loom census healthy_before=refs_total:22588,refs_home:13239,gone:151,lost:168 healthy_after=identical verdict=byte_identical
loom census launcher_broken_before=gone:151,lost:168,under_ceiling:yes launcher_broken_after=gone:152,lost:169,under_ceiling:no
loom discovery source_files_before=4784 source_files_after=2097 removed=2687 fixture_list_before=77 fixture_list_after=77 names_declared_by_hand=5
loom roster tier=lap guards=68 green=68 red=0 tree_moved=no caravan_suite=green
loom qa ITINERARY=A+ REDS=A+ dated_path_exclusions=A+ autonomous-loop.seed=A frames_pushed=0
file tools/fixtures/dated_path_exclusions.sh discovery reads instruments only; five test-body plants declared by hand
file tools/fixtures/dated_path_exclusions_control.sh ordinary_source_free proven in the pen; the .rye leg moved to an instrument so it can still red
file tools/d/dated_path_witness.rish the new leg gated rather than printed
file tools/l/launch-claude-chapter.rish the loop launcher names a document that exists
file recursion-prompts/seed/autonomous-loop.seed.md the recipe names a document that exists
file construction/REDS.md %268 booked and closed; headline rewritten from the measured spine
file construction/ITINERARY.md Now refilled, prior lap folded to one row
think The closing --hot roster refused: 11 reds across 68 guards, tree_moved=no. Standfast. Every one of them kept its own words under construction/standing-equipment-reds/, which is the %266 repair earning its keep on its first real reds -- I read eleven root causes off disk rather than reproducing eleven failures.
think Seven traced to the pull rather than to my work. The rebase brought the second pier's ten commits in, and git fires no pre-commit hook during a replay while no roster runs on arrival: a new root room arbor/ carried no verdict in template-manifest.bron (sow), its author.sh sat at 100644 with a shebang and took the exec-bit ratchet 57 -> 58, its README.md offered no path home, a session log stood flat with no index row, and both generated pages were stale because the hook that rewrites them never fired. Booked %269.
obs A pull is an arrival, and an arrival is not a commit. Every wall this tree owns fires at git commit or at a roster run, and a rebase is neither. The far pier ran its own hooks on its own tree; nobody ran this roster over the MERGED tree, which is the only tree that ever held both halves. The round-open pull is REDS %252's seated mitigation and it moves work in without asking a guard about the result.
think Classified arbor personal rather than template, on the manifest's own fail-safe at line 14 -- when a path's contents are uncertain, it is personal -- because deciding a brand-new room INTO the public seed is a projection call with privacy stakes and I have not read its files. dexter already waits the same way. That closes the red without crossing anything.
think Four of the eleven were mine. The card carried Git nib PENDING, which nib_honesty and remember_git_nib both refuse and rightly -- I replaced a real hash with a placeholder rather than leaving the prior nib to be amended. REDS %268 and %269 carried the pin to 29,279 bytes against 24,576, so declared_ceiling and e123 both red; folded %266, %267 and %268 to one shelf, since all three are a guard whose silence was never earned, and the pin reads 21,771.
think The eleventh is the one worth keeping: dated_path read refs_lost=170 against a ceiling of 168, because my own REDS row and session log must QUOTE the broken path to explain it, and a row naming a lost reference is itself one. The ledger already knows this shape at %245, %246 and %253. A ceiling only falls, so I listed the invented basename with its forwarding address rather than raising anything -- it names nothing by construction, since the molt invented it and no file ever wore it. Census back to 168.
loom standfast roster_reds=11 from_the_pull=7 mine=4 rooted_from_evidence_files=11 reproduced_by_hand=0
loom repair reds_pin_before=29279 reds_pin_after=21771 bound=24576 rows_folded=266,267,268 census_before=170 census_after=168 ceiling=168_unchanged
think The re-run left one red: readme_reach, broken_in_living=2, both in the shelf I had just written. Folding a row one directory deeper leaves its relative links pointing one level short -- and REDS %247 booked exactly this class, named the loom that would end it, and closed with the words "the next one depends on whoever runs it remembering this row." I did not remember it. Booked %270, re-anchored both links to ../../ and verified each resolves from the shelf's own directory, readme_reach GREEN.
obs A row that ends in "the fix is for someone to remember" has forecast its own second firing. The detection side is genuinely sound -- readme_reach bit this inside the round both times, before either shipped -- so what %270 asks for is prevention, and it is a lap's work needing nobody's word.
loom roster_second_pass guards=68 green=67 red=1 red=readme_reach broken_in_living=2 tree_moved=no
think The pre-send fetch found xykj61 six commits further on, so the round re-integrated a second time. Four conflicts: three were session-logs/README.md rows, resolved as a union newest-first, which is the shared-pin rule the Grain foundation already names -- 119 rows against 119 flat logs, no duplicates, no orphans.
think The fourth was semantic and worth the reading. tools/k/kyri_receipt_witness.rish: the far pier asserts three exact strings, mine asserts a floor citing REDS %235 -- read the floor, never the count, so a lap adding a sixteenth claim edits no guard. I asked kyri/receipt.rye what it actually prints rather than choosing a side: claims_floor=15 ... seven refusals bitten, two palettes paired. The far pier's first string, fifteen claims held, names something the module no longer emits; its other two are live. Resolved as a union -- my floor plus both of their real claims -- and proved it both ways: the witness runs GREEN, and the upstream string appears zero times in the real output, so their version would have refused.
obs Re-verified with the FULL roster after the rebase rather than the intersecting subset. %269 is two hours old and says a rebase runs no hook and no guard; taking the narrow path on the same day I booked that would be the lesson unlearned.
think The send met the fast-forward refusal, which is the compare-and-set working, and the third re-integration hit REDS %230's own collision -- the ledgers disagree from %233 onward. Upstream carries %233-%237 at stamps 20260826.014404 through .140601; this pier carries %233-%243, seven of them at 20260825.235254, and its %240-%242 wear the same three stamps upstream gave %233-%235. Same reds, different numbers, third firing after %230 and %252.
obs Aborted rather than renumbered. %230's rule for a specific collision is seated -- the earlier stamp keeps the number -- and %248 already parked exactly this package for Keaton, with the card saying plainly that main stays unpushed and each package parks on its own pier/ branch. Applying the rule here means renumbering rows in a ledger this pier did not write and sweeping their citations across both trees, which is the general answer %230 gated. Parking is the seated move and renumbering would be crossing a gate to save a rebase.
obs Booked no new row for it. %230 and %252 are OPEN GATE and this is the same red firing again rather than a new one; spending a third contested number on a ledger whose numbering IS the fault would make the problem the record. Cited rather than re-booked.
loom send xykj61_refusals=1 reintegrations=3 conflicts_resolved=5 parked=refs/heads/pier/a-plant-and-a-wound remotes=xykj61,origin forced=no
recommend keep-going the nearest lap is now the fold loom %270 names -- a tool that re-anchors a folded row's links and names its shelf, twice-asked and needing no word. Behind it the measured list stands unchanged: readlink -f at 4 sites and sed -i at 4, each wanting one helper in tools/fixtures/shell_portable.sh, with the readlink shape question still open -- two of its four sites are Linux-only by construction, so whether the family reads past a host-bound script or repairs it anyway wants deciding before the lap rather than during it. One new thing worth a look on its own lap: phantom_path and the dated-path census divide the path-literal field between them by the rule "resolves somewhere but untracked" versus "resolves nowhere," and this round showed the second half can be silently emptied. The division is sound; what it lacks is a guard that the two together still cover the whole field. Every remaining open red is Keaton's: %230 and %252 want the row-number ruling, %248 the integration word, %255 the send-order choice.
